prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| ## One call per measurement, and one turn for the independent ones
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| 
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| Run `./tools/loop/measure.sh` once, from the change root, before you plan. It prints the change's own numbers as one
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| block — the paths it touches, the checks that pathspec owes (with the command that runs each), the tallies the
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| tree already carries, the project's declared case count with the command that printed it, the gate's own
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| measured timing, and the change's spec scenarios counted against the cases that name them. **It runs none of
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| them**: the gate is run once, by `./tools/loop/gate.sh`, and a number the block lacks is a defect in the block rather
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| than a reason to walk the tree by hand.
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| 
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| **A number the change quotes is the output of a command in its record.** The block's lines are that command's
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| output for the facts it carries, so quote them. A number the block does not carry is one you took yourself, and
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| then the command that took it belongs in the change's evidence beside the number.
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| A number with neither is not a claim: leave it out of the change — a reviewer cannot tell a measurement from a
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| guess, and a wrong number it cannot check turns into a refusal and a whole second round (issue #244).
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| 
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| Two answers the block cannot give by guessing, and asks for instead:
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| 
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| - **Which change.** With one change open it measures that one; with none, the newest archived change. With two
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports|   open it prints `LOOP-MEASURE-CHANGE ambiguous` and names them rather than measuring a stranger's delta — pass
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports|   `--change <name>` then, and on a correction round pass the name of the archived change you are correcting.
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| - **The tallies after a build tree is gone.** The block reads tallies a run already wrote, under the gate's own
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports|   directory and the change's `evidence/`. A `clean` empties the first, and then the block carries no
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports|   `LOOP-MEASURE-TALLY` line at all, which is not the same as a green number: cite the copy in `evidence/`.
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| 
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| Every line of the block carries the prefix `LOOP-MEASURE`. Quote those lines into your report and into the
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| change's evidence instead of re-deriving them. The declared case count is `LOOP-MEASURE-SUITE cases=`; the
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| gate's own timing is `LOOP-MEASURE-GATE seconds=`. Neither is counted again by hand.
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| 
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| **A file you have read once is cited, not read again.** When a later step needs a fact from a file already in
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| your transcript, read the line you have; a second read of the same path is a second model turn and buys
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| nothing. The same holds for a command: a measurement you have taken is quoted, not re-taken. Measured on five
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| items of this loop (issue #246): 8 to 49 of every item's 22 to 79 file reads were a second read of a path the
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| item had already read.
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| 
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| **Independent calls belong in one turn, not in one turn each.** A read, a search, a `git log` and a `jq` that do
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| not need each other's output are one turn's work: issue them together and read the four answers once. Every turn
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| re-reads the whole conversation, so the second call of a pair paid a turn costs the item its context again without
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| buying a fact — measured over this loop's archive (227 items, 2026-11-03): 36 825 tool calls in 33 183 turns,
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| **1.11 calls per turn**, 78 k tokens per turn and 97 % of the spend charged as cache read, and the run reports the
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| ratio per item and per run beside the recorded floor `LOOP_ITEM_CALLS_PER_TURN_MIN`, naming an item that falls
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| below it. A chain of single-call turns is what turns an item into a decision: it is the same budget spent as one
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| long conversation, and the run's turn bound for this role (`LOOP_CHILD_TURNS_MAX_WORKER`) ends the child that runs
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| into it. Batch
prompt#workerGradesItsOwnDiffBeforeItReports| the lookups, and split a phase at its first gate (`tools/loop/phase.sh`) rather than carrying one session past it.
